![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

The validation/classification function `classifyMasterySignalCandidate` should explicitly handle missing, null, or non-object inputs defensively to prevent potential `TypeErrors` and fail closed gracefully with a specific reason code.

```javascript
function classifyMasterySignalCandidate(input = {}) {
  if (!input || typeof input !== 'object') {
    return {
      signal_state: 'suppressed',
      reason_codes: ['invalid_input_record'],
      future_learner_model_consumption_allowed: false,
      runtime_mastery_update_allowed: false,
    };
  }
  const reasons = reasonCodes(input);
  const hasRealAttemptEvidence = input.has_real_attempt_evidence === true;
  const requestsMasteryUpdate = input.requests_mastery_update === true ||
    input.runtime_mastery_update_requested === true;
  const explicitProvisionalGate = input.explicit_mastery_signal_provisional_gate === true;
  const strongSemanticEvidence = input.semantic_signal_strength === 'strong' ||
    input.evidence_posture === 'strong_reviewed_semantic_evidence';
```

<details>
<summary>References</summary>

1. Validation functions should explicitly handle missing, null, or non-object inputs by returning a specific error code (e.g., 'taxonomy_record_required') and failing closed, rather than silently returning empty failures or throwing a TypeError.
</details>

